Java keywords, reserved and literals:
- All are lowercase
- Cannot be used as Identifiers(user defined words)

For example:
- Control program flow: if, else, continue, break, for, do, while, switch, case, default.
- Describe Code Structure: private, public, import, package, class, static, final, return, void.
- Define types: byte, int, double, boolean, var.
- Literal values: true, false, null.
- Work with objects: new, this, super.
- Handle exception: try, catch.

Java identifiers
- Any custom name given to the variables, methods or class by the programmer are called identifiers.

Java Syntax
- Java code is grouped into classes
- A class is the main unit of code in java
- Classes contain variables and methods that fulfill their purpose
- Code blocks are delimited by {} flower brackets
- Statements are terminated with ; semi collen.

Comments
- Comments are used to provide additional information in the program
- Comments explain why that code was written and what is the logic behind it.
- We write comments inside the source code and the compiler ignores it.
// This is the single line comment
/*
This is
multi line comment
*/

The main method:
public static void main(String[] args) {
// Code (variables and methods)
}
- This is the entry point of the Java program or the Java application.
- JVM starts the program from this method

public: public is an access modifier.
        It makes the main() method accessible from anywhere.
        The JVM needs to access this method to start the program, so it must be public.

static: static means the method belongs to the class, not to an object.
        Because of this, the JVM can call main() without creating an object of the class.

void: void is the return type.
      It indicates that the main() method does not return any value.

main: main is the name of the method.
      It is the standard method name that the JVM looks for as the entry point of a Java application.

String[]: String[] is the parameter type.
          It represents an array of String objects.
          This array stores the command-line arguments passed to the program when it is executed.

args: args is simply the parameter name.
      It stands for "arguments," but you can use any valid identifier, such as input, data, myArgs or even your name, try it.
      By convention, most Java programmers use args.

Overall: public static void main(String[] args) is the entry point of a Java application.
         When you run a Java program, the JVM begins execution by calling this method.